/*!
    \example RobotMonitor
    \title Robot Monitor
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {70}
    \brief An RViz-style monitoring and control dashboard for a Nav2 mobile
    robot, combining a live 3D scene, a camera feed, and navigation actions.

    \c{Robot Monitor} is a production-scale example: a live dashboard for a mobile
    robot running the Nav2 navigation stack (a simulated TurtleBot4 / iRobot
    Create3). It renders the SLAM map, costmaps, laser scan, planned path, and
    robot footprint in a Qt Quick 3D scene, streams the robot's camera into the
    UI, and drives the robot with both direct velocity commands and Nav2/docking
    actions. It brings together most of what the other examples show individually:
    many subscribers, a publisher, action clients, TF, and QoS tuning.

    \image RobotMonitor/robotmonitor-idle.png "The Robot Monitor dashboard (bottom) running against a simulated TurtleBot4 in Gazebo (top): connection counters and action status, a live camera feed, the laser scan, local costmap, and robot footprint in the 3D scene, and the Layers panel."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Install the TurtleBot4 simulation (this pulls in \c nav2_msgs,
            \c irobot_create_msgs, and other dependencies):
            \badcode
            sudo apt-get install ros-jazzy-turtlebot4-simulator
            \endcode
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment and launch Qt Creator from that shell.
        \li Open \c examples/RobotMonitor/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li Launch the simulation in a separate, ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 launch turtlebot4_gz_bringup turtlebot4_gz.launch.py \
                nav2:=true slam:=true localization:=false rviz:=false
            \endcode
    \endlist

    \note You can add \c{rviz:=true} to compare the Robot Monitor scene with
    RViz, but running both may strain simulation performance on some systems.

    \section1 Modules and Imported Packages

    The example links the built-in \c NavMsgs, \c GeometryMsgs, and \c SensorMsgs
    modules, and additionally wraps distribution packages on demand. Its
    \c CMakeLists.txt builds \c IMPORT_PACKAGES conditionally with
    \c{find_package(... QUIET)}, so the application still builds and runs when an
    optional package is missing:

    \badcode
    qt_ros2_configure_target(appRobotMonitor
        CAPABILITIES PUBLISHER SUBSCRIBER ACTION
        MODULES QtRos2NavigationMessages QtRos2GeometryMessages QtRos2SensorMessages
        IMPORT_PACKAGES ${_optional_import_packages}  # nav2_msgs, irobot_create_msgs, ...
    )
    \endcode

    Wrapped packages appear under the \c QtRos2.Imported namespace — here
    \c QtRos2.Imported.Nav2Msgs (Nav2 actions) and
    \c QtRos2.Imported.IrobotCreateMsgs (dock/undock actions). Because those
    types may be absent, the example isolates them in a separate QML file loaded
    through a \c Loader (see \l{Actions and Reactive State} below), so the app
    degrades gracefully rather than failing to load.

    \section1 One Node, Many Entities

    All ROS entities are declared as children of a single
    \c{Node { nodeName: "qt_robot_monitor" }} in \c MainViewModel.qml. The node
    also computes connection \c statistics by reducing over its \c entities and
    testing them against the base types (\c Ros2SubscriberBase,
    \c Ros2PublisherBase, \c Ros2ActionClientBase) — which is what drives the
    "connected vs total" counters in the status row.

    \section1 Subscribers and QoS Presets

    Different ROS topics need different Quality of Service, and the example
    deliberately picks a preset to match each one via the \c QualityOfService
    factory:

    \list
        \li The map is \e latched (published once and kept), so its subscriber
            uses \c{QualityOfService.transientLocal()} to receive the last value
            on connect.
        \li The laser scan and camera are high-rate sensor streams, so they use
            \c{QualityOfService.sensorData()} (best-effort).
        \li Costmaps, the planned path, the footprint, and \c cmd_vel use the
            reliable default.
    \endlist

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.NavMsgs
    import QtRos2.SensorMsgs

    OccupancyGridSubscriber {
        id: mapSubscriber
        topic: "map"
        qos: QualityOfService.transientLocal()   // latched map
    }

    LaserScanSubscriber {
        topic: "scan"
        qos: QualityOfService.sensorData()        // best-effort sensor stream
        onMessageReceived: _d.updateInstanceList(message)
    }
    \endqml

    The laser scan handler converts polar ranges into XY points and feeds a Qt
    Quick 3D \c InstanceList, so the whole scan is drawn as instanced geometry.
    The map and costmaps are rendered by an \c OccupancyGrid component, the planned
    path (\c nav_msgs/Path) and footprint (\c geometry_msgs/PolygonStamped) as
    polylines.

    \section1 The 3D Scene as a TF Tree

    The scene mirrors the robot's TF tree: nested Qt Quick 3D \c Node items whose
    \c position and \c rotation are bound to live transforms. Transforms come from
    a single \l{Qt ROS2 Transforms QML Types}{FrameTransformer}, which maintains a
    TF buffer from its own \c /tf and \c /tf_static subscriptions and emits
    \c transformsChanged whenever the tree updates. \c TFManager.qml reads each
    edge from it:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Transforms

    FrameTransformer { id: tfXform }

    // Inside TFManager, refreshed on every transformsChanged:
    if (frameTransformer.canTransform(parent, child)) {
        const tr = frameTransformer.lookupTransform(parent, child).transform
        root[prefix + "_p"] = GeometryHeleper.toVector3d(tr.translation)
        root[prefix + "_q"] = GeometryHeleper.toQuaternion(tr.rotation)
    }
    \endqml

    Each \c lookupTransform(parent, child) returns the child frame expressed in
    its parent — exactly the local transform a nested scene-graph node needs.

    \section1 Bridging the Camera Image

    The camera feed shows how a C++ item can consume a ROS message value type
    directly. \c RosImageItem is a \c QQuickItem with a single property typed as
    the \c sensor_msgs/Image value type:

    \code
    Q_PROPERTY(Qtros2SensorMsgs::Image image READ image WRITE setImage NOTIFY imageChanged)
    \endcode

    In QML, the item is bound straight to the subscriber's message — there is no
    separate C++ subscription; the bridge delivers the message as an assignable
    value type:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.SensorMsgs

    ImageSubscriber {
        id: cameraSubscriber
        topic: "oakd/rgb/preview/image_raw"
        qos: QualityOfService.sensorData()
    }

    RosImageItem { image: cameraSubscriber.message }   // in VideoView.qml
    \endqml

    On the scene-graph thread the item maps the ROS \c encoding string to a
    \c QImage format, wraps the message bytes without copying, and uploads them as
    a texture. This is the general pattern for high-throughput data: let C++
    consume the strongly-typed message value, not a raw ROS subscription.

    \section1 Actions and Reactive State

    Driving the robot and commanding navigation use a publisher and three action
    clients. Direct driving publishes \c geometry_msgs/TwistStamped:

    \qml
    TwistStampedPublisher { id: cmdVelPublisher; topic: "cmd_vel" }

    function publishVelocity(vel) {
        rosNode.cancelCurrentAction()          // manual driving preempts navigation
        cmdVelPublisher.publish({ twist: vel })
    }
    \endqml

    The action clients — \c NavigateToPoseActionClient (Nav2) plus
    \c DockActionClient and \c UndockActionClient (iRobot Create3) — live in
    \c Nav2IrobotActions.qml, which is brought in through a \c Loader so the app
    still runs when those imported packages are unavailable:

    \qml
    Loader {
        id: _actions
        source: "Nav2IrobotActions.qml"
        onLoaded: item.node = rosNode
    }
    // Every access is guarded: _actions.item?.navigateToPose(pose, yaw)
    \endqml

    Although \c sendGoal() returns a JavaScript promise that resolves with the
    result, this example intentionally does \e not await it. Instead it drives the
    UI reactively from each client's \c state (an \c Ros2ActionClientBase action
    state) and \c feedback properties, and cancels with the imperative
    \c cancelGoal(). This "bind to state" style is often the cleanest way to keep
    a dashboard in sync with several concurrent operations.

    \section1 The UI at a Glance

    \list
        \li A top status row shows connection counters and live action status
            (navigation distance/time remaining, dock/undock progress).
        \li The center is a top-down Qt Quick 3D view: pan/zoom/rotate with the
            mouse, and Ctrl+drag to draw a navigation-goal arrow.
        \li A collapsible \e{Camera Feed} panel hosts the live video.
        \li A right-hand side panel offers a \e Drive tab (velocity D-pad plus
            dock/undock) and a \e Layers tab (per-layer visibility, opacity, and
            color scheme).
    \endlist

    \sa {TurtleSim Controller}, {R6 Robot Teach Pendant}, {Qt ROS2 Transforms QML Types},
    {Teleoperation and Remote Control}
*/
